        </div>

        <div class="mt-16 bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-8">
          <h2 class="text-2xl font-black text-white mb-4">The Bottom Line</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            <strong class="text-white">Read the failure before buying another tube.</strong> A bead split cleanly down the middle was stretched past its movement rating. A bead torn raggedly was bonded to the back of the joint and could not stretch at all. Those are different problems with different fixes.
          </p>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            Match movement capability to the joint — polyurethane or silicone at 25 to 50 percent for anything that moves seasonally, not acrylic latex at twelve. Fit backer rod so the caulk bonds on two sides only and sits at about half the joint width in depth. Remove every trace of the old bead first.
          </p>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            And if a joint fails every year with water staining nearby, consider that it may be draining rather than leaking. Weep holes, siding lap bottoms and the undersides of sills and drip caps are meant to be open — sealing them causes the damage they were designed to prevent.
          </p>
        </div>

